{"about":"e0959a2b912e796cf946820383cadac756231541281c4842450e120b92d3dc24","body":"The conjecture claims that preferring a theory for its 'boldness' avoids induction, yet the very act of selecting the bold theory for action presupposes that past survival under severe tests is a reliable indicator of future utility or structural fidelity. If corroboration provides no reason to believe the theory is true or likely to succeed in new applications, then choosing it over a less corroborated but equally bold alternative becomes arbitrary. The preference implicitly relies on the inductive assumption that a theory which has withstood attacks in the past is a better instrument for navigating the future than one that has not, thereby smuggling induction back in through the pragmatic backdoor of 'efficiency.'\n[would settle: A demonstration that Popper's criterion for 'severity' can distinguish between theories without relying on any background assumption that past performance correlates with future reliability.]","kind":"mini.criticism.v1"}